+*   **Precision:** The method uses a fractional number of bits per value, which may introduce quantization errors [S1, S2].
 
 ## Operating characteristics
-*   **Time Complexity:** Decompression is performed on-the-fly in a single render pass by the GPU [S1, S2].
-*   **Memory Complexity:** Low; does not require storing the entire decompressed volume in memory [S1, S2].
-*   **Measured Performance:** For 3D textures, $6\times6\times6$ blocks yield 0.56 bps, and $3\times3\times3$ blocks yield 4.74 bps [S1, S2].
-*   **Scaling:** Scalable via different block-size configurations covering a wide range of bit rates [S1, S2].
-*   **Strengths:** Hardware-supported, flexible block sizes, competitive with DXT, ETC2, and PVRTC [S1, S2].
-*   **Limitations:** 3D specification status was uncertain at the time of writing; lossy compression [S1, S2].
-*   **When to Use:** Real-time rendering of time-varying volumetric datasets where bandwidth is limited and hardware supports ASTC [S1, S2].
-*   **When Not to Use:** When lossless compression is required or hardware does not support ASTC decompression [S1, S2].
+*   **Time Complexity:** Decompression is performed in real-time on the GPU in a single render pass, allowing direct access to voxel coordinates without full decompression [S1, S2].
+*   **Memory Complexity:** Does not require additional memory to store the decompressed data, as the entire volume is not decompressed at once [S1, S2].
+*   **Performance:** Results are claimed to be competitive with advanced hardware-supported formats like DXT, ETC2, and PVRTC [S1, S2].
+*   **Strengths:** Scalable block sizes, hardware support, low memory footprint for rendering [S1, S2].
+*   **Limitations:** 3D hardware support was not fully standardized at the time of the sources [S1, S2].
+*   **When to Use:** Real-time rendering of time-varying volumetric datasets where bandwidth is limited and on-the-fly decompression is required [S1, S2].
+*   **When Not to Use:** When full decompressed volume access is needed or when hardware support for the specific block sizes is unavailable [S1, S2].
 
 ## Background
-ASTC was introduced by Nandakumar et al. [NLP* 12] as a flexible hardware-supported technique [S1]. It is an adaptation of BTC that shares similarities with S3TC [S2]. The method was proposed to improve over industry standards such as DXT [Bro01], ETC2 [SP07], and PVRTC [Fen03] [S1]. The 3D part of the specification was under evaluation for inclusion in next OpenGL releases as of 2012 [S1]. Fraedrich et al. [FBS07] proposed a general approach for applying sequential data compression schemes to blocks for GPU decoding, which influenced the on-the-fly decoding concept [S1, S2].
+ASTC was introduced by NLP* in 2012 [S1, S2]. It is an adaptation of Block Truncated Coding (BTC) that supports hardware-accelerated random access, sharing similarities with S3TC [S2]. It was designed to improve upon industry standards such as DXT, ETC2, and PVRTC [S1, S2]. The method was proposed for inclusion in OpenGL, though the 3D specification was still under evaluation at the time of the sources [S1, S2].
 
